{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"alias":"dedicated-lowkey-seal","assignee":"Joe Oakhart","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe platform verifies one artifact and publishes a different one, and nothing reconciles the two.\n\n`_clean_checkout` at `services/orchestration/temporal_activities/checks.py:38` copies the agent workspace working tree and runs the project's checks against that copy. Its docstring states that the uncommitted working tree \"is the change under review, so the checks run against exactly what the pull request will carry\". Nothing enforces that claim.\n\nThe publish path never reads the workspace. `collect_changes` at `services/orchestration/materialization.py:177` reduces the workspace to a `dict[str, str]` of path to text, and `commit_and_push` at `adapters/real/git_host.py:120` clones the repository into a fresh temporary directory, writes those files, commits and pushes. The verified artifact and the published artifact are only as equal as that dict is faithful.\n\nThe dict is not faithful. `collect_changes` skips every porcelain entry whose status contains `D` at line 185, so a file the agent deleted is never removed on the branch. It reads each path with `read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")` at line 189, so a binary file raises `UnicodeDecodeError` instead of committing. It passes `--no-renames` at line 179, which turns a rename into an add plus a dropped delete, publishing the new path and leaving the old one in place. File mode changes are lost because the mapping carries no mode.\n\nThree further mechanisms each assume one commit per execution, which the CI repair loop violates.\n\n- `mediator.py:115` builds a `SideEffectRef` whose target is `f\"{repo}:{request.branch}\"`. `side_effect_key` at `services/coordination/idempotency.py:39` hashes task, execution, operation and target, so every commit within an execution produces one key. `_gated` at `mediator.py:183` returns the stored result before `gate.admit` runs, so the sequence gate never sees the later calls.\n- `adapters/real/git_host.py:127` searches the branch history for a commit carrying the trailer `execution_id: <id>` and returns early when it finds one. `adapters/real/local_git_host.py:124` repeats that search and `adapters/mock/git_host.py:98` keys an `applied` set on the execution id.\n- The `commit_and_push` activity at `services/orchestration/temporal_activities/pull_request.py:287` re-materializes at `req.commit`, the head the previous push created. `_ensure_checkout` at `materialization.py:145` sees a head that differs, then runs `checkout -f -B`, `reset --hard` and `clean -fd`, destroying the first repair's uncommitted edits.\n\nA pilot run against `archive-massgov-pfml` made the consequence concrete. Execution `intake-83bd3367` called `commit_and_push` three times, at sequences 202, 205 and 207. All three returned commit `37fd44e7a7`, and `agent_platform.side_effects` holds a single completed row for the execution. The second repair changed `__snapshots__/status.test.ts.snap`, checks then reported 628 suites and 772 snapshots passing, and the workflow promoted the pull request. That snapshot file is absent from the promoted branch, and running the promoted head in the same `node:24.16.0-bookworm` image with the same `npm ci` fails 6 tests and 4 snapshots.\n\n## Approach\n\nPublish the workspace itself rather than a reconstruction of it.\n\nThe workspace is already a git checkout with an authenticated remote. `materialization.py:88` resolves its clone URL through `build_git_host(resolved).source_location`, and `source_location` at `adapters/real/git_host.py:81` returns exactly the authenticated URL the adapter pushes with. So `origin` inside the workspace can already push, and no new credential plumbing is required.\n\nThe workspace stages its own change, and the adapter commits that staged tree in the workspace and pushes the branch. The temporary clone and the file mapping both go away. Deletions, renames, binary content and mode changes survive because git carries them rather than a text mapping.\n\nContent addressing replaces the execution-scoped dedup. Staging yields a tree hash from `git write-tree` that is distinct for each repair attempt and identical for a retry of the same attempt. That is the idempotency the trailer search was reaching for, expressed without a counter and without assuming one commit per execution. The adapter compares the remote branch head's tree against the staged tree and reports a replay when they match, which also makes a retry after a push that already landed safe rather than a non-fast-forward rejection. The same tree hash becomes the `SideEffectRef` target, so the ledger gains per-attempt identity from content that already exists rather than from a synthetic sequence.\n\nThe re-materialization problem then resolves itself. Once the commit exists in the workspace, the head the workflow carries forward is the workspace head, so `_ensure_checkout` takes its early return at `materialization.py:151` and never reaches the reset. Developing against an arbitrary branch or commit keeps working, because the workspace is materialized once at the requested base and afterwards only moves forward by local commits.\n\nA post-push verification closes the gap that allowed a promotion on unverified content. After pushing, the activity checks that the remote branch head equals the workspace head and that the workspace is clean. Any future divergence between the verified artifact and the published one fails the run instead of promoting a pull request.\n\nThe commit disables signing and hooks for the same reason ADEVX-130 did. The workspace is a clone of the client repository, so it carries that repository's hooks and any inherited `commit.gpgsign`, either of which produces no commit while reporting success.\n\nThe alternative of keeping the file mapping and adding a discriminator to the ledger key was rejected. It would not have worked, because `adapters/real/git_host.py:127` would still find the execution trailer from the first commit and decline to push, and it would leave the deletion, rename, binary and mode losses in place.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `adapters/interfaces/git_host.py` replaces `CommitRequest.files` with `workspace` and `tree`.\n- `adapters/real/git_host.py` commits and pushes from the workspace, drops the temporary clone and the trailer search, and reports a replay from a tree comparison against the remote head.\n- `adapters/real/local_git_host.py` commits from the workspace and updates the local branch, dropping its worktree and trailer search.\n- `adapters/mock/git_host.py` records the published tree per branch and reports a replay when the tree is unchanged, dropping its execution-keyed `applied` set.\n- `services/orchestration/materialization.py` gains staging that returns the tree hash and whether it differs from the workspace head, `build_commit_request` carries the workspace path and tree, and `collect_changes` is removed.\n- `services/integration/github/mediator.py` targets the `SideEffectRef` for `github.commit` on the published tree.\n- `services/orchestration/temporal_activities/pull_request.py` stages before committing and verifies the remote head and a clean workspace after pushing.\n- `services/orchestration/bounded_task_pipeline.py` decides the no-changes outcome from the staging result rather than an empty file mapping.\n- Tests: `tests/unit/test_commit_publish.py` is new. `tests/unit/test_local_git_host.py`, `tests/integration/test_workspace_materialization.py`, `tests/integration/test_github_mediator.py`, `tests/integration/test_github_real_backend.py` and `tests/integration/test_bounded_task_end_to_end.py` move to the workspace contract.\n\n## Grounded assumptions\n\n- The workspace can push without new credentials. `materialization.py:88` sets `remote_url_for` from `build_git_host(resolved).source_location`, and `adapters/real/git_host.py:81` returns `self._remote_url(repo)`, the same value `commit_and_push` passes to `clone` and `push` today.\n- The workspace is a full checkout on the activity worker, not in the sandbox. `_clean_checkout` at `checks.py:38` reads it with `shutil.copytree` from the worker, and `materialization.py` runs `git` against it on the worker.\n- The agent's edits reach the workspace as a patch, so deletions are expressible there. The loss happens later, in `collect_changes`.\n- Every caller of `collect_changes` goes through `build_commit_request`. `materialization.py:199` is its only call site outside tests, so removing it does not strand a caller.\n- All three git hosts carry the execution-scoped dedup, so all three need the change. The interface declares `commit_and_push` at `adapters/interfaces/git_host.py:134` and the implementations are at `adapters/real/git_host.py:120`, `adapters/real/local_git_host.py:122` and `adapters/mock/git_host.py:94`.\n- The sequence gate does not block the additional pushes. `_admissible` at `services/coordination/sequencing.py:30` admits a sequence ahead of the current one, and the pilot's three calls carried 202, 205 and 207.\n- `_ensure_checkout` already early-returns on a matching head and branch at `materialization.py:151`, before the `checkout -f -B` at line 161 and the `reset --hard` and `clean -fd` at line 169, so no new branch is needed to preserve the working tree.\n- `CommitResult` needs no shape change. It carries `repo`, `branch`, `commit_sha`, `pushed` and `replayed` at `adapters/interfaces/git_host.py:46`, and the tree comparison populates `replayed` the same way the trailer search did.\n- A commit made in a client checkout can fail silently. ADEVX-130 established that an inherited `commit.gpgsign` without a usable key, and a `pre-commit` hook exiting non-zero, each produce no commit, and that `-c commit.gpgsign=false` with `--no-verify` commits in both cases.\n\n## Testing\n\nHappy path: a workspace holding a modification is staged, committed and pushed, the remote branch head matches the workspace head, and the returned sha is the workspace head.\n\nFidelity: a deleted file is absent from the published tree. A renamed file appears only at its new path. A binary file is published byte for byte. A file whose mode changes to executable keeps that mode. Each of these fails against the current file mapping.\n\nRepair loop: two successive staged changes in one execution produce two distinct commits, both reach the branch, and the second contains the first. The workspace working tree survives a re-materialization at the head the previous push created.\n\nIdempotency: staging the same content twice yields the same tree and the second publish reports a replay without pushing. A publish retried after the push already landed reports a replay rather than failing on a non-fast-forward. Two different trees within one execution produce two distinct `SideEffectRef` targets.\n\nFailure and edge paths: a workspace with no staged difference reports no change and creates no commit. A workspace carrying an inherited `commit.gpgsign` and a failing `pre-commit` hook still commits. A push that leaves the remote head different from the workspace head fails the activity. A workspace left dirty after the commit fails the activity.\n\nLive end to end: re-running the pilot ticket against `archive-massgov-pfml` through a repair cycle promotes a pull request whose head passes the same checks the platform reported.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A file deleted in the workspace is absent from the published tree, verified by `tests/unit/test_commit_publish.py`.\n- [ ] A file renamed in the workspace appears only at its new path in the published tree, verified by the same test module.\n- [ ] A binary file is published byte for byte rather than raising, verified by a test publishing bytes that are not valid UTF-8.\n- [ ] A file whose mode becomes executable retains that mode in the published tree, verified by a test reading the mode from `git ls-tree`.\n- [ ] Two staged changes in one execution produce two distinct commits on the branch, with the second descending from the first, verified by a test asserting the branch has two commits.\n- [ ] Publishing the same staged tree twice pushes once and reports `replayed` on the second call, verified by a unit test.\n- [ ] A publish retried after the remote already holds the tree reports `replayed` and does not fail on a non-fast-forward, verified by a unit test.\n- [ ] Two different staged trees in one execution produce two different `SideEffectRef` targets, verified by a test over `services/integration/github/mediator.py`.\n- [ ] A workspace with no staged difference yields the no-changes outcome and creates no commit, verified by a unit test over the staging result and a `bounded_task_pipeline` test.\n- [ ] A workspace configured with `commit.gpgsign` and a `pre-commit` hook exiting non-zero is still committed, verified by a test that executes the publish against such a repository.\n- [ ] A re-materialization at the head the previous push created preserves uncommitted workspace edits, verified by a test in `tests/integration/test_workspace_materialization.py`.\n- [ ] The activity fails when the remote branch head does not equal the workspace head after pushing, verified by a unit test.\n- [ ] The activity fails when the workspace is not clean after committing, verified by a unit test.\n- [ ] `collect_changes` is removed and no caller remains, verified by `grep -rn \"collect_changes\" --include=*.py .` returning nothing outside this ticket's own history.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A live run of the pilot ticket through at least one repair cycle promotes a pull request whose head passes the checks the platform reported.\n  provenance: environment=local-colima-k3s; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — the pilot executes the shipped workflow against the client repository over the same Temporal path a deployment uses\n- [ ] `ruff check services tests scripts adapters` exits 0.\n- [ ] The full unit and integration suite passes.","id":"e869-50b3-8e21-45bf","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["adevx"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"ADEVX-131: Publish the verified workspace commit instead of rebuilding it from a file mapping"},"env_id":"8c3ef925-fbdc-4d88-8d59-f391cf59568c","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789695364669934001,"uuid":"c9dcb104-2e0b-443a-8acb-8b2c16828194"}